bout somebody being  dogmatic we mean that they have some  sort of preconceived notion or belief  that they are relying upon now there's  an older conception of dogma that says  that it's it's something that you need  to believe in order to make any sort of  progress and Heidegger here wants to  question something that has been framed  as a dogma if we're going to do  philosophy or indeed if we're going to  do any sort of discipline or inquiry at  all  this has to do with with being itself so  he tells us what is this Dogma that  being is the most universal and empty of  concepts he's going to go on and tell us  that this resists every attempt at  definition this most universal and thus  indefinable concept doesn't need any  definition everybody uses it constantly  already understand what's meant by it by  it and he tells us this is what troubled  or thus what troubled ancient  philosophizing and kept it so by virtue  of its obscurity has become to us in the  present obvious clear as day  and if you begin asking about being  people will say oh you're making a big  dinners mistake Heidegger wants to take  us back beyond that and he talks about  three prejudices he identifies three  particular ways in which people are  setting a stumbling block in in the way  of even conceiving that there could be a  question of being so the first prejudice  is that being is the most universal  concept and I highlight that term  Universal there because Heidegger is  going to stress to us that there's more  than one way of being universal he  quotes a few nice passages from Greek  and Latin you know to Olin SDK the  lumati stop Hanten allude called primo  cottage some apprehension a test n  squeeze intellectus included to an  omnibus cweickum quick quiz apprehended  and then summarizes these by saying an  understanding of being has already  contained in everything we apprehend in  beings  now if being is a universal concept what  does that mean for us to be universal  let's think by way of analogy to these  pieces of chalk right three individual  pieces is there some sort of universal  that encompasses them yes chalk and you  know we could say writing implement or  we could say hard substance mineral or  something along those lines and what  we're doing there is we're moving from a  lower level of individuality and  specificity up to higher and higher  levels of what we might nowadays call  classes or types but in ancient  philosophy these were called species and  then genus now is being a genus some  people have treated it as if it is such  Heidegger says no being is not a genus  and as a matter of fact Aristotle knew  this already so it doesn't have the kind  of universality that genus does he says  being does not delimit the highest  region of beings in so far as they are  conceptually articulated according  according to genus and species so if we  want to say that being is in fact the  most universal it's not Universal in the  way that we're most often considering  universality so we want to put this  prejudice aside the second prejudice  that he discusses is that the concept of  being is indefinable now think about how  we define things now go beyond just  providing a verbal definition or you  know let alone some sort of tendentious  stipulative definition that happens to  you know fit whatever you're trying to  do at a particular time think about what  it means when we really do  in fact define something we say what it  is we provide a verbal formula that in  some respect is is supposed to capture  the essence of whatever the thing is why  wouldn't we be able to define being he  says this conclusion is drawn from its  highest universality and correctly so if  definition happens through the proc  Magina sand specific difference if if we  define by saying first what kind of  thing it is and then saying how it's  different from other things and we can't  do that with being now can we but again  we need to think is this the only way to  define 